<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Li Thai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1368</span> · Also known as Maha Thammaracha I, Lithai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Li Thai, who reigned as Maha Thammaracha I, was king of Sukhothai from about 1347 to 1368. A devout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> Buddhist and scholar, he is credited with writing the Traiphum, or Three Worlds, a treatise on Buddhist cosmology that shaped Thai thought for centuries. He promoted Sinhalese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and built temples and Buddha images, though Sukhothai's political power was declining against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/li-thai.jpg" alt="Li Thai" width="320" height="470" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: พระราชวชิรเมธี รองเจ้าคณะจังหวัดกำแพงเพชร เจ้าอาวาสวัดพระบรมธาตุนครชุม · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahathammaracha_I.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
